ExifTool handles this transparently — when you write -SubSecDateTimeOriginal='1970:01:01 00:00:00.001', it splits the value into the date part (written to DateTimeOriginal) and the sub-second part (written to SubSecTimeOriginal). 

The Samsung SEFT format (in detail)
The Samsung Extended Feature Trailer (SEFT) is a proprietary binary structure appended after the JPEG's EOI marker (FF D9).  It was reverse-engineered by Brian K. Reid (Hacker Factor, 2024).

Structure:

SEFT Header:
  Offset 0x00:  "SEFT" magic (4 bytes)
  Offset 0x04:  Version (2 bytes) = 107
  Offset 0x06:  Record count (2 bytes) = 6
  ...

Index Table (one entry per record):
  Each entry:
    Offset +0x00:  Record type (4 bytes LE)   e.g., 0x00000a01
    Offset +0x04:  Record offset (4 bytes LE)  (negative = from end of file)
    Offset +0x08:  Record length (4 bytes LE)
    Offset +0x0C:  Data length (4 bytes LE)

Record 0x0a01 (Image_UTC_Data):
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Field name length: 14 (0x0E)                      │
  │  Field name:      "Image_UTC_Data" (14 ASCII bytes) │
  │  Value length:      13 (0x0D)                      │
  │  Value:             "1700513181420" (13 ASCII)     │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

The 13-byte value is the Unix timestamp in milliseconds as an ASCII string. ExifTool reads this, converts it to a human-readable datetime with timezone, and displays it as Samsung: TimeStamp. 

Why exiftool can't write it: The SEFT format is proprietary, undocumented, and exiftool's Samsung plugin only implements a reader for it. There's no write path. 

Finding and patching the exact bytes
Method 1: exiftool -v3 to find the offset 

exiftool -v3 original.jpg 2>&1 | grep -A5 "Image_UTC_Data"

Output:

  SamsungTrailer_0x0a01Name = Image_UTC_Data
  - Tag '0x0a01-name' (14 bytes):
    3c8a6b: 49 6d 61 67 65 5f 55 54 43 5f 44 61 74 61  [Image_UTC_Data]
  TimeStamp = 1700513181420
  - Tag '0x0a01' (13 bytes):
    3c8a79: 31 37 30 30 35 31 33 31 38 31 34 32 30      [1700513181420]

The value is at offset 0x3c8a79 — 13 bytes of ASCII. 

Method 2: sed one-liner

sed -i 's/Image_UTC_Data1700513181420/Image_UTC_Data0000000000001/g' original.jpg

This works because the name and value are contiguous in the binary — the 14-byte name is immediately followed by the 13-byte value. Since both are ASCII, a simple string replace is safe.

Method 3: xxd (for precision)

# Find the offset
exiftool -time:all -v3 original.jpg 2>&1 | grep "0x0a01'"

# Patch at exact offset (e.g., 0x3c8a79)
printf '0000000000001' | xxd -r -p > /tmp/patch.bin
dd if=/tmp/patch.bin of=original.jpg bs=1 seek=$((0x3c8a79)) count=13 conv=notrunc

Method 4: GUI hex editor (bless, imhex, 010 Editor) 

Open the file, search for Image_UTC_Data, and replace the 13 digits after it with 0000000000001. 

Why the value is 0000000000001 and not 0
The target is 1970:01:01 00:00:00.001 — that's 1 millisecond after the epoch, not the epoch itself. 

Unix epoch (ms) = 0          →  1970:01:01 00:00:00.000
Target             = 1        →  1970:01:01 00:00:00.001  ✓

The 13-digit padding (0000000000001) is required because the field is a fixed-width 13-byte ASCII field — Samsung always writes 13 digits (enough for timestamps up to year 2286). 

